    <h1 style="text-align: center;">Corporate Leave Management System (CLMS)</h1>
    <h2 style="text-align: center; color: #555;">Project Presentation Document</h2>
    <p><b>Team Members:</b></p>
    <ol>
        <li>[Name 1]</li>
        <li>[Name 2]</li>
        <li>[Name 3]</li>
    </ol>
    <hr/>
    <h3>1. Introduction</h3>
    <p>The Corporate Leave Management System (CLMS) is a robust, enterprise-grade web application designed to streamline the process of leave tracking and approval within an organization. It eliminates manual paper-based requests, reduces errors in leave calculation, and provides transparency for employees, managers, and HR administrators.</p>
    
    <h3>2. How the App Works (Workflow)</h3>
    <p>The application operates on a structured hierarchical workflow:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Request Initiation:</b> An employee logs into their portal, checks their available leave balance (Casual, Sick, or Earned), and submits a leave application specifying dates and reason.</li>
        <li><b>Managerial Review:</b> The employee's designated manager is notified (via their dashboard). The manager reviews the request against team availability and either approves or rejects it, adding necessary remarks.</li>
        <li><b>Automated Deduction:</b> Upon manager approval, the system's database automatically deducts the approved days from the employee's specific leave balance using atomic, transaction-safe operations.</li>
        <li><b>HR Oversight:</b> HR Administrators can view the overall leave trends, monitor organization-wide metrics, and manage user accounts and allocations throughout this process.</li>
    </ol>
    
    <h3>3. Technology Stack</h3>
    <p>The application is built on a reliable and scalable Java-based stack:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Backend Environment:</b> Java 17+, Jakarta EE (Servlet 6.0, JSP 3.1)</li>
        <li><b>Database Architecture:</b> MySQL 8.0+</li>
        <li><b>Connection Pooling:</b> HikariCP (for optimized database connection performance)</li>
        <li><b>Frontend Interface:</b> HTML5, CSS3, Bootstrap 5.3 for responsive design, Vanilla JavaScript, and JSTL (JSP Standard Tag Library)</li>
        <li><b>Build & Dependency Management:</b> Maven</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3>4. Core Modules</h3>
    <p>The system is cleanly divided into four distinct modules based on user roles and responsibilities:</p>
    <h4>A. Authentication & Security Module</h4>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):</b> Ensures users can only access portals relevant to their designation.</li>
        <li><b>Data Security:</b> Implements BCrypt password hashing to secure user credentials.</li>
        <li><b>Session Management:</b> Securely validates sessions to prevent unauthorized access to protected routes.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h4>B. Employee Module</h4>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Dashboard:</b> Provides a real-time summary of available leave balances (Casual, Sick, Earned).</li>
        <li><b>Leave Application:</b> A user-friendly form to request time off.</li>
        <li><b>Leave History:</b> A comprehensive log of past and pending leave requests, including manager remarks.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h4>C. Manager Module</h4>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Team Management Dashboard:</b> Allows managers to view all pending leave requests from their direct reportees.</li>
        <li><b>Approval Workflow:</b> Capability to approve or reject leave applications with mandatory remarks.</li>
        <li><b>Team Availability:</b> Visibility into team schedules to ensure adequate coverage before approving leaves.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h4>D. HR Admin Module</h4>
    <ul>
        <li><b>User Management:</b> Ability to onboard new employees, update profiles, and assign employees to respective managers.</li>
        <li><b>Leave Allocation:</b> Capability to allocate or modify annual leave balances for employees.</li>
        <li><b>Organization Analytics:</b> View organization-wide leave metrics and generate reports for audits.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3>5. Key Features Used</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Transactional Consistency:</b> Utilizes strict database transactions to ensure that leave approvals and balance deductions happen atomically, preventing data discrepancies.</li>
        <li><b>Responsive UI/UX:</b> The Bootstrap-powered frontend ensures the application works seamlessly on desktop and mobile devices.</li>
        <li><b>MVC Architecture:</b> Follows the Model-View-Controller design pattern for clean separation of concerns, making the codebase highly maintainable.</li>
        <li><b>Connection Pooling:</b> Uses HikariCP to manage a pool of database connections, significantly reducing latency during high user traffic.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3>6. Conclusion</h3>
    <p>The Corporate Leave Management System serves as an automated, secure, and highly efficient tool that brings transparency and speed to an otherwise tedious administrative process. The use of Java EE and MVC architecture ensures that the system is scalable and ready for future enterprise enhancements.</p>
</body>
